# <%= it.scope %>/telemetry

Tracing and error reporting for every app here: everything in
[`@mfeflow/telemetry`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@mfeflow/telemetry), plus the collector address and service
names of this company, and one helper that applies them. `/otel` is re-exported too.

## What to fill in

`src/defaults.ts` holds the settings:

- `otlpEndpoints`: the collector's OTLP/HTTP traces URL of `dev`, `staging` and `prod`. They are empty; put your
  addresses in.
- `SERVICE_PREFIX`: put in front of every service name, so `checkout` is reported as `<%= it.scope.slice(1) %>-checkout`.

`telemetryOptions(env, service, version, overrides?)` returns the options for `createOtelProvider` and throws if
the collector address of that environment is still empty (unless you pass your own `exporter`). It does not
create the provider itself: `@mfeflow/telemetry/otel` brings in OpenTelemetry, and the shell loads it after the
first render.

In the shell:

```ts
import { initTelemetry, telemetryOptions } from '<%= it.scope %>/telemetry';

initTelemetry(async () => {
  const { createOtelProvider } = await import('<%= it.scope %>/telemetry/otel');
  return createOtelProvider(telemetryOptions('prod', 'shell', '1.4.0'));
});
```

## Settings only, no state

`@mfeflow/telemetry` is a Module Federation singleton, shared by package name. `<%= it.scope %>/telemetry` is not
in that list, so every remote gets its own copy of this package while the one underneath stays shared. That is
fine while this package holds only settings and functions that build things. Keep it that way: no provider,
tracer or flag at module level here. State belongs in `@mfeflow/telemetry` or in the shell.

## In an app

An app that imports `<%= it.scope %>/telemetry` must also list `@mfeflow/telemetry` in its own `package.json` (the shell and every
remote). The preset shares `@mfeflow/telemetry` only when the app can resolve it from its own folder, and pnpm does not
make a dependency of a dependency visible there. Without it the preset cannot see the package, leaves it out of the shared list, and each remote gets its own copy. Telemetry keeps its provider on `globalThis`, so it still works, but the rule is the same for every shared package here.
